## Scripts
### Working with JWTs
Decode a JWT:
```shell
jwt decode jwt_string
```

Decode a JWT as a JSON string:
```shell
jwt decode [-j|--json] jwt_string
```

Encode a JSON string to a JWT:
```shell
jwt encode [-A|--alg] HS256 [-S|--secret] 1234567890 'json_string'
```

Encode key pair payload to JWT:
```shell
jwt encode [-A|--alg] HS256 [-S|--secret] 1234567890 [-P|--payload] key=value
```

## Working with Github
### 1Password CLI Plugin for gh
* Interactive `gh` is a `gh()` function in `~/.zsh_aliases`: the real binary when `IAI_GH_DIRECT` is set, otherwise `op plugin run -- gh` (Touch ID). Non-interactive shells never see it.
* Check `GH_TOKEN` first. A repo whose `.envrc` exports it needs no `op` call; run `gh` directly. `I-AI-Corp/iai-explorer` (local checkout `~/projects/iv-living-archive`) does this, and also puts a `gh` shim with a cached token on PATH.
* Only when `GH_TOKEN` is unset, run `export GH_TOKEN=$(op plugin run -- gh auth token)` once per session. Never prefix individual `gh` commands with it.

### Two GitHub accounts

`omairiai` is work, `omair-inam` is personal. `GH_TOKEN` is the work account, and on a personal repo such as `omair-inam/dotfiles` it reports `viewerPermission: READ`, which is just public access. Read that field before assuming a token can open a PR. The real `gh` binary at `/opt/homebrew/bin/gh` has an empty keyring, so the work token is the only credential a non-interactive shell has.

### What a background session can and cannot do

Push works, the GitHub API does not. Establish both before promising to land anything on a personal repo.

* `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` points at launchd and holds no identities. Export `$HOME/Library/Group Containers/2BUA8C4S2C.com.1password/t/agent.sock` and `git fetch`, `push`, and `-S` signing all work with no prompt.
* `op plugin run` fails with `interactive IO not available`, because it wants a TTY.
* `op` itself is authenticated as a service account scoped to the work vaults, so a personal-vault item returns `isn't a vault in this account` or `a vault query must be provided when this command is called by a service account`. The personal `gh` token is unreachable from here.
* So: commit, sign, and push, then hand over `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/new/<branch>` and the PR title and body. Do not burn turns hunting for a credential that this session cannot reach.

### Signing outside the work repo

`~/.local/share/chezmoi` sets `gpg.format` and `user.signingkey` but not `commit.gpgsign`, so a plain `git commit` there silently produces an unsigned commit. Always pass `-S`. Verification is separately broken: `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` is unset, so `%G?` prints `N` on a correctly signed commit and `E` on the signed commits already on `main`. Confirm signing by looking for a `gpgsig` header in `git cat-file commit HEAD`, never by `%G?`. Commits on that repo are authored `Omair-Inam Abdul-Matin <omairkhawaja@gmail.com>` while its config sets `user.name=omair-inam`, so pass `--author` to match history.

### Editing a skill under ~/.claude

`~/.claude` is not a git repo, but most of it is chezmoi-managed, so "open a PR" means `omair-inam/dotfiles`. Edit the live file, then `chezmoi add` the exact paths to bring the change into `home/dot_claude/...`; chezmoi encodes the executable bit as an `executable_` filename prefix, so the repo mode stays `100644`. That working tree carries a lot of unrelated modifications, so branch from `origin/main` in a worktree under the already-ignored `.worktrees/`, copy in only the files you touched, and check `git diff origin/main --stat` before committing.

### 1Password Touch ID / SSH Signing
* Git commit signing, `gh` auth, and `git push` all require 1Password Touch ID approval
* If a command fails with `error: 1Password: failed to fill whole buffer` or similar 1Password auth errors, **do NOT retry automatically**
* Instead, tell the user: "1Password Touch ID authentication failed. Please let me know when you're ready and I'll retry the command."
* Only retry once after the user confirms — do not loop

### Pull requests
- Add header below to all pull request descriptions:

